WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.820
Good morning, John.

00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:03.080
We are really, really lucky.

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:05.560
Here’s a thing that’s kind of hard for
me to think about: Over the last ten years,

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:10.980
we have had 1400 chances to try something
new, and in one way or another, sometimes

00:00:10.990 --> 00:00:15.470
big sometimes super super tiny, we have tried
something new every single video.

00:00:15.470 --> 00:00:20.220
Sometimes I humped things, sometimes you ate
toilet paper, sometimes I sang songs, sometimes

00:00:20.230 --> 00:00:21.230
we went to the developing world.

00:00:21.230 --> 00:00:25.210
We’ve done this publicly, with an audience
of varying sizes, and the success of those

00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:26.939
attempts has been judged.

00:00:26.939 --> 00:00:30.410
Judged by the number of views those things
got, what kind of audiences they attracted,

00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:33.670
whether we liked doing them, how our friends
and families felt, whether we thought those

00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:38.710
videos were interesting and good, and also
by people telling us what they thought in the comments.

00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:42.539
In those 1400 chances, a lot of those ideas
were duds, but some of them were amazing.

00:00:42.539 --> 00:00:46.550
I made a video with a person I met at a weird,
tiny research museum in Missoula, which led

00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:47.879
to The Brain Scoop becoming a thing.

00:00:47.879 --> 00:00:51.539
And you and I made videos on the French Revolution
and on the circulatory system that led to Crash Course.

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:55.780
It’s impossible to underestimate the effect
that this has had, not just on the kind of

00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:58.000
stuff I make, but who I have become as a person.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:01.699
If you count SciShow and Crash Course, I’ve
had thousands of chances to try and figure

00:01:01.699 --> 00:01:05.330
out who I am in front of good people who I
respect.

00:01:05.330 --> 00:01:10.070
And the kindness of their judgement, their
empathy and compassion and their wit and silliness,

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:15.090
and of course my own affinity for those things,
has made me a kinder, more enthusiastic, more

00:01:15.090 --> 00:01:16.090
compassionate person.

00:01:16.090 --> 00:01:20.610
We’ve had thousands of chances to evolve
our content, and thousands of chances to evolve

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:26.070
ourselves, and this has helped me clear up
the murky waters of what it is that I really care about.

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:27.340
It’s helped me find me.

00:01:27.350 --> 00:01:31.490
And a lot of themes have emerged over the
years of us doing this, like areas of fertile

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:36.610
soil where I found stuff that I like making,
that I’m good at making, and that I feel

00:01:36.610 --> 00:01:38.810
like is good for the world, and that people
like watching.

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:41.590
Sometimes those are formats, like kinds of
videos we can make.

00:01:41.590 --> 00:01:43.560
Sometimes they’re just ideas.

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:45.009
Ways to consider the world.

00:01:45.009 --> 00:01:48.680
For me, the most important time that happened
was when you distilled so much of what the

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:53.280
world needs right now into three words when
you said “imagine others complexly.”

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:58.890
Because it’s really hard, but whenever I
do it, I feel better, and I am better.

00:01:58.890 --> 00:02:04.799
A lot of the projects that we have created
have Wikipedia pages, but this idea has a Wikipedia page.

00:02:04.799 --> 00:02:07.149
It’s part of Wikipedia’s essays on civility.

00:02:07.149 --> 00:02:09.000
That is unusual, and it’s amazing.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.560
But of course, it isn’t just other people,
it’s the whole world.

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:16.520
We have to imagine the world in its complexity
as well, and not see that as a burden,

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:17.691
but as an opportunity.

00:02:17.691 --> 00:02:20.770
Like, a wonderful wonderful thing that we
get to spend our whole lives doing.

00:02:20.770 --> 00:02:24.460
And when I look back at the kind of content
that we make and that we've helped other people make,

00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:27.320
that thing is always there in all of
it.

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:30.220
And when I think about what I want to make
in the future, if that isn’t there, that

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:34.770
appreciation for and excitement about the
fact that the world is not as simple as it

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:38.111
might seem, I find myself not wanting to do
that thing, and sometimes I don’t even know

00:02:38.111 --> 00:02:42.430
why until I check in, and I’m like, yeah,
it’s just ‘cause it doesn’t do the thing

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:43.310
that I want to be doing.

00:02:43.310 --> 00:02:47.400
That thing, imagining complexly, has become
a really big part of what we do and who we are.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:51.520
Now, most of the people watching this probably
don’t know that we have a company that produces

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:55.940
a bunch of different content, and it has a
name, and it’s called EcoGeek.

00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:59.420
And it’s called EcoGeek because I started
a blog in 2005 called EcoGeek, and it’s

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:03.590
the reason I started my first company, and
when I did that, I had an LLC, and I figured

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:06.950
I might as well make all of my other stuff
happen under the same thing, and it’s been

00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:10.840
so long now that I’ve been using this name
that it doesn’t really apply anymore.

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:15.120
So this week, after years of thinking about
this and never being able to settle on a name,

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:18.870
we are changing our name of the company that
produces the content.

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:24.230
We have called it Complexly, because the world
is not simple, and we will never truly understand

00:03:24.230 --> 00:03:26.750
our universe or each other or even ourselves.

00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:31.380
The process is as powerful as the outcome,
and the more you know, the more wonderful

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:32.200
knowing becomes.

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:37.520
Because we want to imagine things complexly,
and because we want to help other people do it, too.

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:39.180
John, I’ll see you on Tuesday.

